428:**Paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000).** The round-2 ml-methodology reviewer noted that the B = 200 bootstrap resamples marginal means, not paired differences (§9.2 #15). We re-run with B = 2000 on the paired seed-level differences for the two headline contrasts:
451:The best context-blind policy (EDUCATIONAL|EMAIL|bid 0.3, +$252.41) beats every deployable context-aware agent, including `cam_multisignal` (+$206.13), `cam_multisignal_learned` (+$205.39), and `cam_learned` (+$155.38), in 7 of 9 environments. This is a **benchmark-construction limitation** (§9.2 #25): the environment's reward function does not penalize wrong-channel assignments, so the cheapest channel dominates. The multi-signal classification gain (87.5% → 98.7%, +$50 learned-vs-learned) is measured *within* the context-aware ladder; the channel-cost arbitrage shows that a context-blind policy on a cheap channel can outperform a context-aware policy on an expensive channel. The paper's contribution is the *relative* value of multi-signal classification (F9, §8.6), not a claim that context-aware agents beat all context-free policies. **Resolved (Section 8.10):** A channel-dependent reward condition (mismatch penalty 0.5) drops cb_edu_email_030 from +$252.41 to +$132.72 while cam_multisignal is nearly unchanged (+$206.13 -> +$205.77); the arbitrage disappears.
455:A matched-family ablation within the NCC family (§8.6) shows the multi-signal gain is signal-driven, not classifier-capacity-driven. But NCC is the generative family — the clusters are Gaussian, and NCC is Bayes-optimal for Gaussian clusters. A stronger classifier on the same signals could narrow or close the multi-signal advantage (§9.2 #13). We benchmark two standard ML classifiers on the same 2000-sample calibration set:
482:**Macro-F1 (§9.2 #11):** Match rate (accuracy) is unbalanced under the skewed class distribution (exploration 35%, consideration 30%). Macro-F1 averages per-class F1, penalizing classifiers that never predict minority classes. Results (seed 1, 200 scenarios):
496:**Common Random Numbers (§9.2 #10):** The benchmark seeds a single global `random` stream per seed, so agent *k* sees the stream after agents 1…k−1 have consumed it — the *context* is paired but the *decision noise* is not. CRN (per-agent RNG, same contexts) changes profits by <$3 for all stochastic agents (baseline: +$0.81; noisy50: −$2.62; noisy80: +$0.86). All deterministic agents (cam_multisignal, cam_learned, etc.) are unaffected. CRN does not change any conclusion; the paired t-test remains valid (§9.2 #10).
504:To test whether the benchmark can falsify a wrong mapping (Section 9.2 #1), we construct an agent with perfect 3-signal classification (98.9% situational match) but a deranged action mapping (EXPLORATION->PROMOTIONAL, CONSIDERATION->CRISIS_RESPONSE, DECISION->EDUCATIONAL, CRISIS->LOYALTY, OPPORTUNITY->COMPARISON, RETENTION->URGENT). Results: 0.4% match against the true IDEAL_ACTION, profit -$268.88 (worse than the random baseline, -$170.03). The contrast vs cam_multisignal is +$475.01 (p = 5.23e-96, d = 29.28). Perfect classification with a wrong mapping is catastrophic, not merely suboptimal: the mapping itself carries the value, and the benchmark is falsifiable via profit.
512:We replace uniform epsilon-corruption with class-conditional noise (Section 9.2 #16, Section 10.3 #13): crisis<->decision and retention<->exploration are the documented confusable pairs. At 10%/20%/30% corruption rate (only these 4 of 6 classes are corrupted; consideration and opportunity keep clean labels), cam_multisignal_learned's match drops from 98.7% to 96.3%/93.0%/89.2% and profit from +$205.39 to +$198.20/+$186.59/+$172.27. The 30% class-conditional contrast is -$33.11 (p = 1.13e-34, d = -2.13). Class-conditional noise is less damaging than uniform noise at the same rate (uniform 30% retention noise drops match to 84.9%, Section 8.5.2) because only 4 of 6 classes are corrupted.
516:We test for slow monotone drift (Section 9.2 #17) with Spearman rho of profit vs seed index (1..50) and a Geweke z-score (first 20% vs last 50%). cam_multisignal: rho = -0.301 (p = 0.034, marginally significant), Geweke z = 2.04 (borderline). baseline: rho = 0.189 (p = 0.188), z = -1.05. cam_learned: rho = -0.176 (p = 0.221), z = 0.39. The marginal cam_multisignal trend (rho = -0.301) reflects slightly higher profits in early seeds, not a systematic drift; the Geweke z = 2.04 is borderline (|z| < 1.96 would be non-significant). The paired t-tests (which use per-seed differencing) are robust to this trend. Conclusion: no strong convergence concern, but the marginal trend is reported for transparency.
520:To de-confound perception from bidding (Section 9.2 #4, Section 10.3 #4), we run NoisyCAM with flat bidding (bid = 1.0, no situation-based multiplier) at p_correct in {0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0}. Results: profit increases monotonically from +$128.79 (p=0.5, 58.1% match) to +$294.27 (p=1.0, 100% match). Per-seed Spearman rho(profit, p) = 0.998 (min 0.943). This confirms the perception->profit dose-response is not an artifact of bid policy: even with flat bidding, better perception yields higher profit. The p=1.0 profit (+$294.27) equals situation_only (+$294.27), confirming that flat bidding is optimal when perception is perfect; the oracle's 2.0x crisis multiplier is suboptimal (Section 8.16).
524:The default crisis response is CRISIS_RESPONSE with a 2.0x bid multiplier (Section 6.5). We test three alternatives (Section 9.2 #23, Section 10.3 #17): pause (bid = 0.0), normal (bid = 1.0, no multiplier), and counter (2.0x, current default). Results (50 seeds x 200 scenarios): in the default environment, pause earns +$225.10, normal +$215.62, counter +$206.13. In the crisis_heavy environment (25% crisis situations), the gap widens: pause +$243.79, normal +$192.87, counter +$141.93. Pausing (bid = 0) is consistently better than counter-messaging (2.0x): the 2.0x crisis multiplier overbids in low-reward crisis situations. This finding does not invalidate the action mapping (CRISIS->CRISIS_RESPONSE is still correct) but suggests the bid multiplier should be calibrated, not hardcoded.
528:The `quality_score` signal is a decoy (uniform 0.5-1.0, uncorrelated with the situation-linked `channel_quality`); classifiers read `context.channel_quality` directly (Section 9.2 #24, Section 10.3 #18). We test whether replacing the decoy with the real `channel_quality` (and adding `competitive_density` as a signal) changes results. With `sensing_fidelity = True`, all four classifier agents produce identical profits: cam_multisignal +$206.13, cam_multisignal_learned +$205.39, cam_learned +$155.38, cam_inferred +$104.26. This confirms the sensing layer is decorative: classifiers already use the real context scalars, not the signals list. Multi-modal sensing claims should be understood as testing the classifier on the three real signals (intent, competitive density, channel quality), not the sensing infrastructure.
532:The §8.12 bandit baselines were **offline-trained** — the Agent ABC has no reward-feedback loop (§10.3 #12, §9.2 #13). Yet §8.11 showed the mapping itself carries the value. We therefore ask: **can an agent learn the situation→action mapping from observed profit alone, within a single 200-step episode?** Two online agents mirror `cam_multisignal` exactly — same 3-signal classifier, same channel rule (`SITUATION_CHANNEL[sit_est]`), same heuristic bid — and differ *only* in the action decision, which comes from a learned policy instead of the fixed `IDEAL_ACTION` table:

