153:**Positioning vs contextual bandits:** Contextual bandits (Li et al., 2010; Agrawal & Goyal, 2013) are the closest algorithmic neighbor — they select actions from context signals and are deployed at scale in ad platforms. CAM differs in three respects: (1) bandits optimize a scalar reward from a fixed arm set; CAM introduces a **situation taxonomy** (6 archetypes) that mediates context→action mapping; (2) bandits learn online from observed rewards; CAM-Sim evaluates offline classification quality against ground-truth labels; (3) bandits do not model **situational awareness** (Endsley Levels 1–3) — they are context-aware but not situation-aware. A systematic comparison with contextual-bandit baselines is deferred to future work (§10.3).
344:**F9 (multi-signal awareness — the framework's core claim, confirmed):** Adding competitive density and channel quality to the intent-only signal raises match and profit. **The fair comparison is learned-vs-learned:** `cam_multisignal_learned` (98.7%, +$205.39) vs `cam_learned` (87.5%, +$155.38) — a +$50.01 gain (p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96, 95% CI [+$46.5, +$53.5]), confirming that multi-signal structure is recoverable from data alone. **Against the hand-tuned threshold classifier** (`cam_inferred`, 75.7%, 4-of-6 classes, +$104.26), the gain is larger: +$101.87 (p = 5.4e-46, d_z = 7.88) — within $3.87 of the oracle (+$210.00). The advantage is **universal on the handicapped comparator**: `cam_multisignal` beats `cam_inferred` in all 9 environments (§8.4), with the largest swings exactly where intent-only classification collapses — `crisis_heavy` (−$66.8 vs +$141.9, a +$208.7 swing) and `retention_heavy` (−$15.5 vs +$237.2, +$252.7). The learned-vs-learned comparison is not reported per-environment (§10.3 #11). Mechanism: intent alone conflates crisis↔decision (both high intent) and retention↔exploration (both low intent); competitive density and channel quality resolve both ambiguities. This is the operational validation of CAM Layer 1 (*sense multi-modal context signals*): multi-signal awareness is not a marginal improvement but the difference between sub-oracle and near-oracle performance.
508:Two offline-trained bandit baselines (Section 10.3 #12): (a) epsilon-greedy (classify situation via 3-signal NCC, epsilon=0.1 random action, else Q-argmax = ideal action) and (b) a LinUCB-style linear profit model (ridge regression per action type on (1, intent, comp, quality) -> profit, trained on 2000 calibration contexts). Results: epsilon-greedy earns +$169.91 (90.1% match, exploration tax -$36.22 vs cam_multisignal, p = 2.95e-31, d = -2.25); LinUCB earns +$91.42 (75.6% match, -$114.71, p = 4.13e-48, d = -7.35). Both underperform cam_multisignal (+$206.13). The linear profit model's poor performance confirms the reward landscape is nonlinear: a ridge regression on 3 signals cannot capture the situation->action mapping that the NCC classifier exploits.
512:We replace uniform epsilon-corruption with class-conditional noise (Section 9.2 #16, Section 10.3 #13): crisis<->decision and retention<->exploration are the documented confusable pairs. At 10%/20%/30% corruption rate (only these 4 of 6 classes are corrupted; consideration and opportunity keep clean labels), cam_multisignal_learned's match drops from 98.7% to 96.3%/93.0%/89.2% and profit from +$205.39 to +$198.20/+$186.59/+$172.27. The 30% class-conditional contrast is -$33.11 (p = 1.13e-34, d = -2.13). Class-conditional noise is less damaging than uniform noise at the same rate (uniform 30% retention noise drops match to 84.9%, Section 8.5.2) because only 4 of 6 classes are corrupted.
520:To de-confound perception from bidding (Section 9.2 #4, Section 10.3 #4), we run NoisyCAM with flat bidding (bid = 1.0, no situation-based multiplier) at p_correct in {0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0}. Results: profit increases monotonically from +$128.79 (p=0.5, 58.1% match) to +$294.27 (p=1.0, 100% match). Per-seed Spearman rho(profit, p) = 0.998 (min 0.943). This confirms the perception->profit dose-response is not an artifact of bid policy: even with flat bidding, better perception yields higher profit. The p=1.0 profit (+$294.27) equals situation_only (+$294.27), confirming that flat bidding is optimal when perception is perfect; the oracle's 2.0x crisis multiplier is suboptimal (Section 8.16).
524:The default crisis response is CRISIS_RESPONSE with a 2.0x bid multiplier (Section 6.5). We test three alternatives (Section 9.2 #23, Section 10.3 #17): pause (bid = 0.0), normal (bid = 1.0, no multiplier), and counter (2.0x, current default). Results (50 seeds x 200 scenarios): in the default environment, pause earns +$225.10, normal +$215.62, counter +$206.13. In the crisis_heavy environment (25% crisis situations), the gap widens: pause +$243.79, normal +$192.87, counter +$141.93. Pausing (bid = 0) is consistently better than counter-messaging (2.0x): the 2.0x crisis multiplier overbids in low-reward crisis situations. This finding does not invalidate the action mapping (CRISIS->CRISIS_RESPONSE is still correct) but suggests the bid multiplier should be calibrated, not hardcoded.
528:The `quality_score` signal is a decoy (uniform 0.5-1.0, uncorrelated with the situation-linked `channel_quality`); classifiers read `context.channel_quality` directly (Section 9.2 #24, Section 10.3 #18). We test whether replacing the decoy with the real `channel_quality` (and adding `competitive_density` as a signal) changes results. With `sensing_fidelity = True`, all four classifier agents produce identical profits: cam_multisignal +$206.13, cam_multisignal_learned +$205.39, cam_learned +$155.38, cam_inferred +$104.26. This confirms the sensing layer is decorative: classifiers already use the real context scalars, not the signals list. Multi-modal sensing claims should be understood as testing the classifier on the three real signals (intent, competitive density, channel quality), not the sensing infrastructure.
532:The §8.12 bandit baselines were **offline-trained** — the Agent ABC has no reward-feedback loop (§10.3 #12, §9.2 #13). Yet §8.11 showed the mapping itself carries the value. We therefore ask: **can an agent learn the situation→action mapping from observed profit alone, within a single 200-step episode?** Two online agents mirror `cam_multisignal` exactly — same 3-signal classifier, same channel rule (`SITUATION_CHANNEL[sit_est]`), same heuristic bid — and differ *only* in the action decision, which comes from a learned policy instead of the fixed `IDEAL_ACTION` table:
580:The pre-registered protocol of §10.3 #24, executed on 2026-11-02 (`paper/cam_sim_v15_llm.py`; results `results/cam_sim_v15.json`, response cache `results/llm_cache/`). Each context is textualized into a natural-language observation — intent strength, competitive density, channel quality, and the verbatim signals list *including the decoy `quality_score`* — and sent to an LLM at temperature 0 whose system prompt states the six situations as **qualitative marketing definitions only** (no numeric centroids, no examples; the full prompt is in the repo for audit). The returned label drives the same fixed pipeline as every CAM agent: `IDEAL_ACTION` action type, `SITUATION_CHANNEL` channel, oracle-style bid. The agent is therefore *identical to `cam_multisignal` except for the classifier* — any profit difference is attributable to classification quality alone. Evaluation runs on the identical per-seed context sequences (pairing-guard asserted against `run_online_env` byte-identically).
632:1. **Reward-design circularity:** The situation→ideal-action table and reward magnitudes are author-designed; the environment cannot falsify the framework's own mapping. External validity requires field validation (Section 10.3). A pre-specified **adversarial-mapping condition** — in which the designer-preferred action is *not* the reward maximizer — **Resolved (Section 8.11):** An adversarial-mapping condition (deranged action mapping, 98.9% classification but 0.4% match) earns -$268.88 vs cam_multisignal's +$206.13 (d = 29.28), proving the benchmark can falsify a wrong mapping.
659:- **Multiplicity:** All p-values survive Bonferroni correction (§7.3). The α-sweep, label-noise, and pacing grids are exploratory — a pre-specified analysis plan with a familywise hierarchy is deferred to the field trial (§10.3).
706:- Level-3 projection (future context) is specified but not benchmarked — a candidate differentiator for future work (§10.3)
721:2. **Findings (Empirical):** Four novel findings — (F3/F8) miscalibrated context-inflated bidding is *worse than flat bidding* (replicates 8/9 environments, survives budget pacing); (F5/F6) systematic classifier bias under distribution shift beats coin-flip adversely, and per-distribution recalibration fixes it robustly to 30% label noise; (F6) match rate is not profit — error *placement* modulates the profit relationship; (F9) multi-signal awareness (intent + competitive density + channel quality) raises match from 87.5% (learned) to 98.7% (learned) and profit by +$50 (p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96); against a hand-tuned classifier, +$102 (p = 5.4e-46). **Standard classifiers (LR, RF) confirm the gain is classifier-family-robust (§8.8):** LR +$65.48 (p = 2.2e-40), RF +$75.98 (p = 3.2e-37); RF-3 achieves 100% match, slightly beating NCC (+$3.66). Note: F9's universality is established on the handicapped comparator; the learned-vs-learned per-environment comparison is deferred (§10.3 #11).
727:### 10.3 Future Work & Field-Validation Design
749:22. **Dynamic environments (partial ✅, Section 8.19; was §10.3 #7):** a mid-episode drift condition (default → crisis_heavy at scenario 100) shows F5 replicates within-episode and **F6's recalibration remedy reverses**: post-drift refit is net-negative over the episode (−$15.08, p = 1.56e-05) because it trades first-half performance for second-half recovery; multi-signal inference is drift-robust (+$102.82/+$71.25 across halves). Remaining: competitor adaptation, multi-period state carryover, drift-detection-triggered windowed refits.
858:*Reproducibility: all numbers regenerate from `paper/cam_sim.py`, `paper/cam_sim_ablations.py`, `paper/cam_sim_extensions.py`, `paper/cam_sim_v14.py`, `paper/cam_sim_v15_llm.py`, and `paper/cam_sim_v16_arms.py` with fixed seeds (§8.21 additionally requires API keys and its response cache — see that section for provider-quota caveats; §8.22 uses seed-level bootstrap CIs with 10k reps, RNG 20261103). Peer reviews: 5 rounds (stats-audit, citation-audit, threats-to-validity, 5× journal-editor, 5× adversarial-reviewer, 5× domain-expert, 5× ml-methodology). Next steps: field-validate with a B2B partner (§10.3 #1); learned bidding (§10.3 #2); competitor adaptation & multi-period carryover (§10.3 #22); fine-tuned LLM classifiers (§8.21 scope).*

