595:**Findings.** (1) **LLM zero-shot classifiers are profit-competitive but balanced-classification-inferior:** raw accuracy (74–83%) is inflated by class imbalance — a no-skill classifier sampling the funnel trio (exploration 36.5% + consideration 30.4% + decision 13.4% = 80.3% of contexts) by base rate scores 80.3% — and all five macro-recalls (50.0–61.3%) sit **below** the hand-tuned `cam_inferred`-style classifier's 65.9% (recalls 100/100/98/5/45/46). The profit advantage over the handicapped single-signal classifier is real but comes from *within-trio* discrimination (big-3 recall 87–100%) plus prior collapse, not from better balanced classification. The lead evidence is the pair that survives the 610-way Bonferroni correction (threshold p < 8.2e-05): glm-4.7 **+$0.07** per scenario (p = 8.9e-06) and Mistral-128B **+$0.09** per scenario (p = 1.2e-08); qwen3.6-35b's +$0.10 (p = 8.1e-03; does not survive 610-way Bonferroni correction) and qwen3.8-27b's −$0.01 (p = 0.70, tie) are not significant after correction, and DeepSeek's −$0.10 per scenario (p = 4.9e-06) is a significant negative result. All five lose decisively to the 2,000-label supervised classifiers (−$33 to −$126 per seed; all p < 1e-04) and to oracle-with-heuristic-bid. **Prompting is not a substitute for labels in this environment.** (2) **The LLMs' blind spots are emergent, structural, and identical across model families:** no model *ever* predicts crisis (glm: 0/94 correct — 83% misread as decision; Mistral-128B: 0/94, 68% as decision; DeepSeek: 0/36, 100% as decision) or retention (glm: 0/195 — 73% as exploration; Mistral-128B: 0/195 — 100% as exploration; DeepSeek: 0/105 — 88% as exploration); qwen3.6-35b: crisis 0/14, retention 0/40; qwen3.8-27b: crisis 0/19, retention 0/61. Mistral-128B is the sharpest collapse: 100% correct on exploration/consideration/decision but it *never leaves those three* — opportunity is misread as consideration 88% of the time, exactly §8.20's costliest cross-axis pair after crisis→decision. All five therefore emit at most 4 of 6 classes — the *same* structural handicap that §9.2 #5 attributes to the hand-threshold `cam_inferred`, here arising from the models' own priors with no threshold rule in sight. (3) **The blind spots concentrate on the classes whose scalar signatures overlap adjacent classes** (crisis intent 0.8 vs decision 0.9; retention intent 0.3 vs exploration 0.2) and exactly on the *costliest* confusion (crisis→decision, −$2.79, §8.20) — while the discriminative rescue sits in the second/third signal's *centroid values* (crisis: channel_quality 0.20 vs ≥ 0.55; retention: competitive_density 0.15 vs ≥ 0.45), which the fitted multi-signal classifier exploits for 98.9%. Zero-shot semantic competence cannot replace the centroid calibration that makes multi-signal inference work. (4) **Cost/practicality:** qwen3.6-35b classified at 0.12 s/call (≈ 274 prompt tokens/call), Mistral-128B at 0.49 s (533.6k prompt + 6.0k completion tokens for 2,000 calls), glm-4.7 at 5.2 s (thinking model); all at $0 API cost (academic/free tiers). The LAN-local DeepSeek-V4-Flash (9.0 s/call, reasoning in a separate field; 340k prompt + 159k completion tokens for 1,000 calls) needs no provider at all — a deployable on-premise agent reaches 74.1% with commodity local hardware. The decoy `quality_score` was included verbatim in every observation; no model's errors concentrated on it. **seed-1 sensitivity (dev-on-test check, computed from the response cache):** dropping seed 1 shifts accuracy only slightly — glm-4.7 79.9→79.6 (−0.3pp), Mistral-128B 80.3→79.7 (−0.7pp), qwen3.6-35b 83.0→79.5 (−3.5pp, only 2 seeds), qwen3.8-27b 75.7→75.0 (−0.7pp), DeepSeek 74.1→73.2 (−0.9pp) — i.e. no pilot overfitting (the largest delta belongs to the 2-seed model).
